<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.1.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00366</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.1.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>c41212d7-b82a-4ab9-a6ed-17f50d1625ec</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para><emphasis>Students will complete the following questions to
  practice the skills they have learned in this lesson.</emphasis></para>
  <para>In 1 - 4, determine if the following points
      are solutions to the given system of equations. </para>
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
      <item>\(\left\{\begin{array}{l}2x-6y=0\\3x-4y=5\end{array}\right.\)</item>
  </list>
  
  <list>
    <item>\((3,1)\)</item>
    <item>\((-3,4)\)</item>
    <item>\((\frac{8}{7}, \frac{6}{7})\)</item>
    <item>\((1, \frac{3}{4})\)</item>
  </list>
  
  <para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\((3,1)\)</para>
  <para>Substitute in the points 
  \((3,1)\) for \(x\) and \(y\) into each equation to see if it is a solution.
  \(2(3)-6(1)=0\), \(6-6=0\), \(0=0\). 
  \(3(3)-4(1)=5\), \(9-4=5\), \(5=5\). </para>
  
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item>\(\left\{\begin{array}{l}x-6y=0\\3x-4y=5\end{array}\right.\)</item>
  </list>
  <list>
    <item>\((3,1)\)</item>
    <item>\((-3,4)\)</item>
    <item>\((\frac{8}{7}, \frac{6}{7})\)</item>
    <item>\((1, \frac{3}{4})\)</item>
  </list>
  <para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\((-3,4)\)</para>
  <para>Substitute in the points 
  \((-3,4)\) for \(x\) and \(y\) into each equation to see if it is a solution.
  \((-3)-6(4)=0\), \((-3)-24=0\), \(-2 \neq 70\). 
  \(3(-3)-4(4)=5\), \((-9)-16=5\),\(-25 \neq 5\). </para>
  
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
    <item>\(\left\{\begin{array}{l}x\;+\;y\;=\;2\\\;y\;=\;\frac{3}{4}x\;\end{array}\right.\)</item>
  </list>
  <list>
    <item>\((3,1)\)</item>
    <item>\((-3,4)\)</item>
    <item>\((\frac{8}{7}, \frac{6}{7})\)</item>
    <item>\((1, \frac{3}{4})\)</item>
  </list>
  <para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\((\frac{8}{7}\), \(\frac{6}{7})\)</para>
  <para>\(\frac{8}{7}\),\(\frac{6}{7}\) for \(x\) and \(y\) into each equation to see if it is a solution.
  \(\frac{8}{7}+\frac{6}{7}=2\), \(\frac{14}{7}=2\), \(2=2\). 
  \(\frac{6}{7}=(\frac{3}{4}\frac{8}{7})\), \(\frac{6}{7}=\frac{24}{28}\), \(\frac{6}{7}=\frac{6}{7}\). </para>
  
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
    <item>\( \left\{\begin{array}{l}4x\;+\;4y\;=\;7\\y\;=\;\frac34x\end{array}\right.\)</item>
  </list>
  <list>
    <item>\((3,1)\)</item>
    <item>\((-3,4)\)</item>
    <item>\((\frac{8}{7}\), \(\frac{6}{7})\)</item>
    <item>\((1, \frac{3}{4})\)</item>
  </list>
  <para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\((1, \frac{3}{4})\)</para>
  <para>\((1,34)\) for \(x\) and \(y\) into each equation to see if it is a solution.
  \(1+\frac{3}{4}=2\), \(\frac{4}{4}+\frac{3}{4}=2\), \(\frac{7}{4}\neq 2\). 
  \(\frac{3}{4}=(\frac{3}{4})(1)\), \(\frac{3}{4}=\frac{3}{4}\). </para>
  
  <para>In 5 - 10, solve the systems of equations by
      graphing.</para>
  
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
      <item>\(\left\{\begin{array}{l}3x\;+\;y\;=\;-3\\2x\;+\;3y\;=\;5\end{array}\right.\)</item>
  </list>
  
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="1" type="enumerated">
    <item>\((-2,3)\) </item>
    <item>\((0,-3)\) </item>
    <item>\((-3,6)\) </item>
    <item>\((4,-1)\) </item>
  </list>
  <para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\((-2,3)\)
  </para>
  <para>Your graph should show the equations intersect at point \((-2,3)\).
  To check your answer, substitute the point into both equations. If both equations are true, your graph is correct and your intersection point is a solution. 
  </para>
  
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
    <item>\(\left\{\begin{array}{l}y\;=\;x\;+\;2\\y\;=\;-2x\;+\;2\end{array}\right.\)</item>
  </list>
  
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="1" type="enumerated">
    <item>\((2,0)\)</item>
    <item>\((-1,4)\)</item>
    <item>\((0,2)\)</item>
    <item>\((-2,0)\)</item>
  </list>
  <para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> \((0,2)\)</para>
  <para>Your graph should show the equations intersect at point 
  \((0,2)\).
  To check your answer, substitute the point into both equations. If both equations are true, your graph is correct and your intersection point is a solution.  
  </para>
  
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
    <item>\(\left\{\begin{array}{l}y\;=\;\frac32x\;+\;1\\y=-\frac12x+5\end{array}\right.\)</item>
  </list>
  

  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="1" type="enumerated">
    <item>\((4,3)\) </item>
    <item>\((0,1)\) </item>
    <item>\((0,5)\) </item>
    <item>\((2,4)\) </item>
  </list>
  <para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> \((2,4)\) </para>
  <para>Your graph should show the equations intersect at point \((2,4)\).
  To check your answer, substitute the point into both equations. If both equations are true, your graph is correct and your intersection point is a solution.  
  </para>
  
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
    <item>\(\left\{\begin{array}{l}x\;+\;y\;=\;-4\\-x\;+\;2y\;=\;-2\end{array}\right.\)</item>
  </list>
  
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="1" type="enumerated">
    <item>\((2,0)\) </item>
    <item>\((-1,0)\) </item>
    <item>\((4,1)\) </item>
    <item>\((-2,-2)\) </item>
  </list>
  <para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\((-2,-2)\)
  </para>
  <para>Your graph should show the equations intersect at point \((-2,-2)\).
  To check your answer, substitute the point into both equations. If both equations are true, your graph is correct and your intersection point is a solution.
  </para>
  
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="9" type="enumerated">
    <item>\(\left\{\begin{array}{l}-2x\;+\;3y\;=\;3\\x\;+\;3y\;=\;12\end{array}\right.\)</item>
  </list>
  
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="1" type="enumerated">
    <item>\((6,2)\) </item>
    <item>\((4,0)\) </item>
    <item>\((3,3)\) </item>
    <item>\((0,1)\) </item>
  </list>
  <para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> \((3,3)\) </para>
  <para>Your graph should show the equations intersect at point 
  \((3,3)\). 
  To check your answer, substitute the point into both equations. If both equations are true, your graph is correct and your intersection point is a solution.  
  </para>
  
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="10" type="enumerated">
    <item>\(\left\{\begin{array}{l}2x\;-\;4y\;=\;8\\x\;+\;3y\;=\;9\end{array}\right.\)</item>
  </list>
  
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="1" type="enumerated">
    <item>\((6,1)\) </item>
    <item>\((4,0)\) </item>
    <item>\((-2,0)\) </item>
    <item>\((8,2)\) </item>
  </list>
  <para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> \((6,1)\) </para>
  <para>Your graph should show the equations intersect at point \((6,1)\).
  To check your answer, substitute the point into both equations. If both equations are true, your graph is correct and your intersection point is a solution. 
  </para>
  
  <para>Solve 11 - 12 by graphing.</para>
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="11" type="enumerated">
      <item>A bakery charges $2 per croissant and $4 per
      muffin. One day, they charge $42 in total and sell 15 baked goods. How many
      croissants and muffins did they sell?</item>
  </list>
  
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="1" type="enumerated">
    <item>5 croissants and 10 muffins</item>
    <item>9 croissants and 6 muffins</item>
    <item>10 croissants and 5 muffins</item>
    <item>8 croissants and 7 muffins</item>
  </list>
  <para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>9 croissants and 6 muffins</para>
  <para>Choose variables, \(x\) for the croissants and \(y\) for the muffins.
  Set up the equation: \(x+y=15\) to represent the total number of baked goods sold on this day.
  Set up the equation: \(2x+4y=42\) to represent the total amount of money made on this day.
  Graph the equations.
  Your graph should show the equations intersect at point \((9,6)\).
  \(x=9\) which represents the croissants and \(y=6\) which represents the muffins.
  They sold 9 croissants and 6 muffins.  
  </para>
  
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="12" type="enumerated">
      <item>A pet store sells mice for $7 each and gerbils
      for $9 each. They sell 17 animals for a total of $137. How many mice and
    gerbils do they sell?</item>
  </list>
  
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="1" type="enumerated">
    <item>15 mice and 2 gerbils</item>
    <item>8 mice and 9 gerbils</item>
    <item>7 mice and 10 gerbils</item>
    <item>6 mice and 11 gerbils</item>
  </list>
  <para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>8 mice and 9 gerbils</para>
  <para>Choose variables, \(x\) for the mice and \(y\) for the gerbils.
    
    Set up the equation: 
  \(x+y=17\) to represent the total number of animals sold on this day.
    
    Set up the equation: \(7x+9y=137\) to represent the total amount of money made on this day.
    
    Graph the equations.
    
    Your graph should show the equations intersect at point \((8,9)\).
    
    \(x=8\) which represents the mice and \(y=9\) which represents the gerbils.
    
  They sold 8 mice and 9 gerbils. </para>
  </section>
</content>
